            <ArticleTitle>Ethics and rehabilitation of the patient with severe burns.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>443-6; discussion 441-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In the case we present, a physician's order for the treatment of a patient with severe burns unfolds into an ethical dilemma for an occupational therapist. Several conflicting thoughts-in terms of the appropriateness and plan for treatment, while trying to maintain the patient's best interest as the central focus-come to mind. We examine the therapist's ethical responsibilities in light of considerations of futility, nonmaleficence, financial costs, and team relations. Several options for responding to the case are suggested.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
